---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import QuickAnswer from "../components/QuickAnswer.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import ProductCard from "../components/ProductCard.astro";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Home Generators for Power Outages",
    "url": "/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Portable Power Stations",
    "url": "/best-portable-power-station/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Home Battery Backup",
    "url": "/best-home-battery-backup/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Battery Backup for CPAP",
    "url": "/best-battery-backup-for-cpap/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-11-03", modified: "2026-11-03" });
const title = "Best Indoor Heater for Power Outage (2026): 7 CO-Safe Picks";
const description =
  "The Mr. Heater Portable Buddy is the indoor heater for power outage use most homes need; a 1-lb cylinder lasts 2 to 5 hours; 7 picks start with a CO alarm.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "mr-heater-portable-buddy",
    "name": "Mr. Heater Portable Buddy Propane Heater",
    "category": "4,000/9,000 BTU, Indoor-Safe, ODS + Tip-Over",
    "badge": "Best Overall Indoor Heater",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Mr. Heater sells it as indoor-safe, fitted with an oxygen depletion sensor and a tip-over shutoff",
      "Two heat settings: low to carry you through the night, high to warm up a cold room",
      "Rated for roughly 225 square feet, about the size of a typical bedroom",
      "Burns a 1-lb propane cylinder and needs no electricity whatsoever",
      "Piezo ignition and a fold-down handle make it simple to light and set in place"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "On high, a 1-lb cylinder lasts only a few hours, so keep a deep supply",
      "The manual requires a fresh-air opening, which lets some heat escape",
      "The front grille runs hot enough to burn skin and scorch fabric"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The go-to indoor emergency heater for most households: it warms one closed-off room, it is easy to run, and it carries the safety devices that matter.",
    "description": "When people talk about indoor propane heat, the Portable Buddy, model MH9BX, is usually the heater they have in mind. Mr. Heater rates it at 4,000 or 9,000 BTU per hour and markets it as indoor-safe. In practice that means two devices: an oxygen depletion sensor that shuts the burner off if oxygen in the room falls, and a tip-over switch that kills the flame if the heater gets knocked over. Those two features are the reason it made this list and a patio heater did not. It burns a single 1-lb propane cylinder. Working from the fuel math, a 1-lb cylinder holds a little over 21,000 BTU, which comes to roughly two to two and a half hours on high and about five on low, so low is the setting you will really live on. Run it in a room you have closed off, open a window or door to the size the manual calls for, keep it at least the stated distance from bedding and curtains, and never light it without a working CO alarm in the same room."
  },
  {
    "id": "mr-heater-big-buddy",
    "name": "Mr. Heater Big Buddy Propane Heater",
    "category": "4,000/9,000/18,000 BTU, Indoor-Safe, Two Cylinders",
    "badge": "Best for Larger Rooms",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Three settings reaching 18,000 BTU, enough for a living room or open family area",
      "Indoor-safe rating, with an oxygen depletion sensor and a tip-over shutoff",
      "Carries two 1-lb cylinders, about doubling the time between swaps",
      "An optional battery-powered fan spreads warmth across a larger room"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "The high setting burns fuel quickly and calls for more ventilation",
      "Larger and heavier than the Portable Buddy",
      "More heater than a single bedroom needs"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Choose it when the warm room is a living room rather than a bedroom, or when the whole household is bunking together in one space.",
    "description": "Think of the Big Buddy, model MH18B, as a scaled-up Portable Buddy. Mr. Heater lists three settings, 4,000, 9,000 and 18,000 BTU, along with the same oxygen depletion sensor and tip-over shutoff, and it holds two 1-lb cylinders on board. Its rating covers spaces up to roughly 450 square feet, enough for a living room or a den where a family can gather with mattresses on the floor. The high setting exists to bring a cold room up to temperature, not to run all night. At 18,000 BTU an hour, two 1-lb cylinders last only a little over two hours by the fuel math, and the manual asks for a bigger fresh-air opening at higher output. Drop to low or medium once the room is warm. The fan genuinely helps in a larger room, and because it runs on D batteries it keeps working with the grid down. If your plan centers on one bedroom, the smaller Portable Buddy is easier and cheaper to keep fueled."
  },
  {
    "id": "mr-heater-little-buddy",
    "name": "Mr. Heater Little Buddy Propane Heater",
    "category": "3,800 BTU, Indoor-Safe, Compact",
    "badge": "Best for Small Rooms",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Compact and light, threading directly onto a 1-lb cylinder",
      "Same oxygen depletion sensor and tip-over shutoff as the larger Buddy models",
      "Modest 3,800 BTU output makes one cylinder last through more of the night",
      "Sized for a bathroom, a small bedroom or a tent"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Only one heat setting, with no high option",
      "The cylinder sits beneath it, so it stands tall and needs a stable, level surface",
      "Not enough output for anything bigger than a small room"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A sensible heater for a small room and a useful backup unit, but its output is too low to be the only heat source in most homes.",
    "description": "Of the indoor-rated heaters in the Buddy line, the Little Buddy, model MH4B, is the smallest. Mr. Heater lists it at 3,800 BTU an hour for spaces up to about 95 square feet, with the same oxygen depletion sensor and tip-over protection as its siblings. The cylinder threads into the base, leaving a unit roughly the size of a large coffee can on a stand. The low output is deliberate. At 3,800 BTU an hour, a 1-lb cylinder runs for roughly five to six hours by the fuel math, enough to carry a small bedroom through much of the night with a single swap. It also suits a bathroom where you only need enough warmth to wash, or works as a second unit beside a Portable Buddy. In real cold it cannot keep up with a large room, and with no high setting it cannot recover a room that has already slipped into the 40s. Read the manual for altitude limits, since oxygen depletion sensors are sensitive to thin air."
  },
  {
    "id": "first-alert-co400",
    "name": "First Alert CO400 Battery-Powered Carbon Monoxide Alarm",
    "category": "Battery CO Alarm, Works Without Grid Power",
    "badge": "Must-Have First Purchase",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Runs on batteries, so it keeps protecting you through the outage",
      "Its electrochemical sensor triggers a loud alarm at dangerous CO levels",
      "Compact enough to sit in the exact room where the heater runs",
      "Cheap enough to buy one for every sleeping area"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "No digital display, so low but rising levels stay invisible",
      "The alarm and its batteries have a limited service life; replace them on schedule"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Buy this before any heater. Every fuel-burning heat source in a living space needs a working battery CO alarm right beside it.",
    "description": "A hardwired CO alarm with no battery backup falls silent as soon as the power drops, which is precisely when people start bringing fuel-burning equipment indoors. The First Alert CO400 runs on batteries, so the state of the grid does not matter to it. It uses an electrochemical sensor and, as UL 2034 alarms are designed to do, sounds a loud alarm when carbon monoxide builds to a dangerous concentration over time. It is the least expensive insurance on this page and the item that matters most. Place one in the room where the heater runs, one in or near each sleeping area, and one on every level of the house. Test it before the season begins and again ahead of any outage you can see coming. Kidde and other brands sell equivalent battery-powered alarms, and some add a digital display showing lower levels before the alarm threshold, a useful extra if you can find one. The essentials are that it runs on batteries and that it sits in the room."
  },
  {
    "id": "sunbeam-heated-throw",
    "name": "Sunbeam Microplush Heated Throw Blanket",
    "category": "Low-Watt Electric, Power Station Friendly",
    "badge": "Best Electric Option",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Uses a small fraction of the power a space heater pulls",
      "Warms the person instead of the room, which is far more efficient",
      "A mid-size power station can keep one going through a night",
      "Earns its keep every winter, not only during outages"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Requires a power station, generator or some other AC source",
      "Does nothing for the pipes or the room temperature",
      "Auto shutoff timers may need resetting during the night"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The smartest way to turn stored battery power into warmth: on a power station, a heated throw keeps someone comfortable for hours on the energy a space heater would burn through in minutes.",
    "description": "Own a power station? In the cold, the most effective use for it is heating people rather than air. A heated throw such as Sunbeam's Microplush model puts warmth right against your body, under a blanket that also holds it in, and heated throws and blankets of this type typically draw well under 100 watts, often far less on low settings. Check the label on the one you buy. The gap is huge. A 1,500-watt space heater drains a 1kWh power station in well under an hour, while a heated throw drawing around 50 watts runs for most of a night on that same battery. Add a sleeping bag or a pile of comforters and you have a bed that stays warm even as the room slides into the 50s. Sunbeam throws include an auto shutoff for safety, which is good, though it means someone may wake up to restart it. Use it on top of you rather than folded or tucked under a mattress, and follow the manufacturer's instructions."
  },
  {
    "id": "coleman-north-rim-sleeping-bag",
    "name": "Coleman North Rim 0 Degree Mummy Sleeping Bag",
    "category": "Cold-Rated Sleeping Bag, No Fuel or Power",
    "badge": "Best Zero-Energy Heat",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Requires no fuel, no electricity and no ventilation",
      "The mummy hood holds heat around your head and shoulders",
      "Rated for far colder conditions than an indoor room will reach",
      "Serves as camping gear the rest of the year"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Some sleepers find the mummy shape confining",
      "Synthetic fill takes up a lot of storage space",
      "Temperature ratings assume ideal conditions; add layers if you sleep cold"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The cheapest and safest way to sleep warm in a cold house. Any household planning for winter outages should keep one cold-rated bag per person.",
    "description": "Few pieces of emergency heat gear are as underrated as a good sleeping bag. It burns nothing and draws nothing, and it turns your own body into the heat source. The Coleman North Rim is a widely sold synthetic mummy bag rated to 0 degrees Fahrenheit, far colder than the inside of a house gets in all but the longest outages. Read that number as a survival limit, not a comfort promise; most people are comfortable well above a bag's rated low. Indoors, that margin is exactly what you want. A family sleeping in cold-rated bags in one closed-off room can get through a night in the 40s with no heater at all, leaving a small propane heater to simply take the edge off in the evening. Put a foam pad or a mattress beneath the bag, because the floor draws heat away faster than air does. If a mummy bag feels too confining, a rectangular bag with a lower rating plus a wool blanket also works."
  },
  {
    "id": "lasko-754200-ceramic-heater",
    "name": "Lasko 754200 Ceramic Space Heater",
    "category": "1,500W Ceramic, Low Setting, Tip-Over Safety",
    "badge": "Best for Generator Owners",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Electric, so there is no combustion and no carbon monoxide",
      "A low setting cuts the draw below the 1,500W maximum",
      "Tip-over and overheat protection",
      "Small, inexpensive and backed by a long track record"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Even on low, it empties a portable power station in hours or less",
      "Only truly practical on a generator or a large home battery",
      "Heats one small space and nothing more"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Worth having only if you own a generator or a large home battery. On a typical portable power station, nothing wastes stored energy faster.",
    "description": "Part of the reason this heater is here is as a caution. The Lasko 754200 is a widely sold small ceramic heater with a 1,500-watt high setting, a lower setting, tip-over protection and an overheat cutoff. As an electric heater it is perfectly good. It is simply a poor partner for most battery backup. At 1,500 watts, a 1kWh power station lasts roughly 35 to 40 minutes once inverter losses are factored in, and the low setting adds only a little more time. It earns its place alongside a generator running outside, a whole-home battery system, or a large portable station that you can recharge from solar or a generator. Electric resistance heat gives off no combustion gases, a real advantage in a closed room, and a ceramic heater with tip-over protection is safer than any open flame. Plug it straight into the station or into a heavy-duty cord rated for the load, keep three feet of clearance from anything that can burn, and never leave it running unattended."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Indoor Heater for Power Outage",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-indoor-heater-for-power-outage/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the safest indoor heater for a power outage?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The safest choices burn nothing: cold-rated sleeping bags, layered clothing and a heated blanket running from a power station. For real room heat without electricity, pick a propane heater its maker rates for indoor use, such as the Mr. Heater Buddy series with its oxygen depletion sensor and tip-over shutoff. Always run it with a battery-powered CO alarm and the ventilation the manual requires."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is the Mr. Heater Buddy really safe to use indoors?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Mr. Heater markets the Buddy series as indoor-safe, and each heater has an oxygen depletion sensor and a tip-over shutoff. Indoor use is reasonable if you follow the manual: open the specified fresh-air gap, keep the stated clearance from anything that burns, use 1-lb cylinders inside, and keep a working battery CO alarm in the room. Many safety agencies advise switching it off before sleep."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does a 1-lb propane cylinder last in a Buddy heater?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A 1-lb cylinder holds a little over 21,000 BTU. Burning 9,000 BTU an hour, that comes to roughly two to two and a half hours; at 4,000 BTU it is about five hours. The 3,800 BTU Little Buddy gets about five to six hours. Cold weather and partly used cylinders cut those times, so keep more cylinders on hand than the math suggests."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I run a space heater on a portable power station?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, though only briefly. A 1,500-watt heater empties a 1kWh station in roughly 35 to 40 minutes, and a low setting stretches that to only about an hour. The same energy goes much further in a heated blanket, which can run for most of a night on that battery. Save space heaters for generators or large whole-home batteries."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I heat my house without electricity?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Focus on one small room rather than the whole house. Close it off, block drafts, cover the windows and bring everyone in. While people are awake, run an indoor-rated propane heater with a CO alarm and the required ventilation, then let cold-rated sleeping bags and layered clothing carry the night. A wood stove or fireplace you already have also works if it is inspected and sound."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use my gas oven or stove to heat the house?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. Gas ovens and ranges are not built to be space heaters, and running one for hours with the door open can produce dangerous carbon monoxide levels while adding a fire risk. Camp stoves and grills of every type fall in the same category. Heat only with appliances labeled for indoor residential use, and keep a battery CO alarm working whenever anything burns indoors."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I buy a kerosene heater for power outages?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Kerosene heaters are not on this list. Whether one may be used indoors depends on state and local rules, the fuel needs careful storage and outdoor refueling, and the heaters demand generous ventilation. For most homes, an indoor-rated propane heater on 1-lb cylinders is simpler to store and run safely. If you are weighing kerosene anyway, read your local codes and the manual first."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "All Metal Brake" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Power & Generators",
    tags: [
      "indoor heater for power outage",
      "emergency heat",
      "propane heater indoor safe",
      "heater without electricity"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <header data-hero class="bg-[#F4F6F8] border-b border-gray-200">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-10 pb-8 lg:pt-14 lg:pb-10">
        <p class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-2 gap-y-1 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest m-0 mb-4">
          <span class="text-[#D93A00]">Buyer's Guide</span>
          <span class="text-gray-300" aria-hidden="true">&bull;</span>
          <span class="text-gray-500">Updated {dates.display}</span>
        </p>
        <h1 class="text-[2.1rem] leading-[1.15] sm:text-5xl font-black tracking-tight text-gray-900 m-0">
          Best Indoor Heater for Power Outage
        </h1>
        <QuickAnswer products={PRODUCTS} />
        <p class="text-lg lg:text-xl text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-5 mb-0">
          The Mr. Heater Portable Buddy is the best indoor heater for a power outage in most homes: an indoor-rated 4,000/9,000 BTU propane heater with an oxygen depletion sensor, sized for a closed room of about 225 square feet. Room size, a battery CO alarm and whether you own a generator or power station change the answer. This guide covers seven picks, runtimes and the safety rules.
        </p>
        <!-- Author & Meta -->
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mt-7">
          <AuthorAvatar size={40} />
          <div class="min-w-0">
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 m-0">Written by <a href="/authors/alex-rivers/" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Alex Rivers</a></p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 m-0">Home Improvement Editor &middot; Updated {dates.display}</p>
          </div>
          <SocialProof users="" />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mt-6">
          <TrustBadge />
          <span class="flex-1 h-px bg-gray-300"></span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-3 mb-0">
          Based on manufacturer indoor-use ratings, heater manuals and published BTU figures, with CO alarm guidance. No paid placements. <a href="/how-we-test/" class="font-bold text-gray-900 underline">Read how we pick</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-8">
      <details open class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl px-5 pt-4 pb-2">
        <summary class="flex items-center gap-3 pb-3 cursor-pointer list-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
          <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-gray-500" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" d="M9 6h11M9 12h11M9 18h11M4.5 6h.01M4.5 12h.01M4.5 18h.01" /></svg>
          <span class="font-bold text-gray-900">In This Guide</span>
          <span class="text-xs font-semibold text-gray-400">13 sections</span>
          <svg class="w-5 h-5 ml-auto text-gray-500 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M6 15l6-6 6 6" /></svg>
        </summary>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-x-6">
              <a href="#short-answer" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">1.</span><span>The Short Answer</span></a>
              <a href="#why-heat-fails" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">2.</span><span>Why the Furnace Stops</span></a>
              <a href="#top-picks" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">3.</span><span>The Seven Picks</span></a>
              <a href="#comparison" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">4.</span><span>Runtime by Option</span></a>
              <a href="#propane-rules" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">5.</span><span>Is Indoor Propane Safe?</span></a>
              <a href="#co-alarms" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">6.</span><span>Carbon Monoxide Alarms</span></a>
              <a href="#power-station-math" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">7.</span><span>Space Heaters on Batteries</span></a>
              <a href="#warm-room" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">8.</span><span>Keeping One Room Warm</span></a>
              <a href="#pipes" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">9.</span><span>Protecting Pipes</span></a>
              <a href="#never-indoors" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">10.</span><span>What Never Comes Inside</span></a>
              <a href="#choosing" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">11.</span><span>Which One to Buy First</span></a>
              <a href="#mistakes" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">12.</span><span>Common Mistakes</span></a>
              <a href="#faq" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">13.</span><span>FAQ</span></a>
        </nav>
      </details>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
