          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Does the low setting help?</h3>

          <p>
            Less than most people expect. A low setting of 750 to 900 watts roughly doubles runtime to about an hour, barely enough to take the chill off a room before the battery is empty, and the room cools again within an hour of the heater stopping. Even a large 4kWh home-backup station buys only a few hours of space heating, and that is energy you probably wanted for the refrigerator and medical equipment.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What should stored power heat instead?</h3>

          <p>
            People, not rooms, which changes the math entirely. A heated throw or blanket typically draws somewhere in the 50 to 100 watt range, so the same 1kWh station runs one for most of a night, and a larger unit runs two or three. Electric mattress pads, heated vests powered by USB power banks and 12V heated car blankets all work on the same principle. If you are shopping for a station with winter in mind, our guides to the <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">best portable power stations</a> and the <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-home-backup/">best power stations for home backup</a> show which sizes cover the fridge, the CPAP and a couple of heated blankets together.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What if you want electric space heat anyway?</h3>

          <p>
            Plan on a generator rather than batteries. A generator running safely outdoors, at least 20 feet from doors and windows, can carry a 1,500-watt heater for as long as the fuel lasts. A solar-charged power station can keep low-watt loads going indefinitely, as our <a href="/best-solar-generator/">solar generator guide</a> explains, but winter sun rarely refills a battery quickly enough to feed a space heater. Spend stored energy where it stretches furthest: blankets, lights, and the devices that keep people informed and safe.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warm-room" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How Do You Keep One Room Warm Without Electricity?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Stop trying to heat the whole house; it is the single most effective change you can make in a cold-weather outage. Choose one room, close it off, move everyone into it, and let a small indoor-rated heater and good bedding do the rest.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which room should you pick?</h3>

          <p>
            Ideally a smaller interior room on the side of the house away from the wind, with few exterior walls and windows. A Portable Buddy rated for about 225 square feet can make a closed bedroom comfortable; it has no chance against an open-plan first floor.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you hold the heat in?</h3>

          <p>
            Shut the doors to every other room and lay rolled towels along the gaps beneath them. Hang blankets over windows and over doorways that have no door, and draw heavy curtains. Keep north-facing windows covered, but open south-facing ones during sunny hours to collect free heat, closing them again before dusk. A tent or blanket fort inside the room, particularly over a bed, holds body heat remarkably well, and children often enjoy it rather than endure it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What should everyone wear and eat?</h3>

          <p>
            Dress for the room instead of heating the room to suit your clothes. Wool or synthetic base layers, a fleece, a warm hat and dry socks matter more than a few degrees of air temperature, and cotton is a poor choice because it holds moisture. Eat regular meals and drink warm drinks if you can heat water safely, since digesting food generates body heat. Keep moving in the daytime; sitting still in a cold room is when people get chilled.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you get through the night?</h3>

          <p>
            Let bedding carry the load. One cold-rated sleeping bag per person, a pad or mattress underneath, and a heated throw on a power station for anyone who runs cold make a complete plan for the sleeping hours. Turn the propane heater off before bed, confirm the CO alarm works, and light the heater again in the morning once everyone is awake. Keep a good battery light within reach so nobody lights a candle; our guide to the <a href="/best-emergency-lantern/">best emergency lanterns</a> covers models that last for days on a charge.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What if someone relies on medical equipment?</h3>

          <p>
            Then the priorities change: anyone on a CPAP, oxygen concentrator or other powered device needs that load covered first and heat second. A CPAP with its heated humidifier off draws little enough to run several nights on a mid-size station, and our <a href="/best-battery-backup-for-cpap/">CPAP battery backup guide</a> explains how to size one. When a long outage, severe cold and medical needs combine, the right move is often to relocate to a warming center or to a relative who has power.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pipes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How Do You Keep Pipes From Freezing When the Power Is Out?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Keep water moving, let room air reach the pipes, and drain the system if the house heads toward freezing. Pipes freeze before people do. Water lines in exterior walls, crawl spaces, unheated garages and under sinks on outside walls are at risk once temperatures near them fall well below freezing, and a burst pipe can cause more damage than the outage itself. The warm-room plan protects people; it does nothing for the plumbing in the rest of the house.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What should you do in the first hours?</h3>

          <p>
            Open the cabinet doors under kitchen and bathroom sinks so room air reaches the pipes, and let faucets on exterior walls drip slowly, hot and cold both. Moving water is much harder to freeze, and an open faucet relieves pressure if ice forms anyway. Find your main water shutoff and make sure it turns before you need it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When should you drain the system?</h3>

          <p>
            When the outage looks long and the house is sliding toward freezing. Shut off the main, open the lowest and highest faucets, and flush the toilets to empty their tanks. Shut off power or gas to the water heater according to its manual before draining lines that feed it. Plumbers recommend this step for vacant houses in winter, and it removes the risk completely.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you thaw a frozen pipe safely?</h3>

          <p>
            Never with an open flame or a propane heater aimed at it. Use warm towels, or a hair dryer once power returns, and work from the faucet end back toward the frozen section so melting water has somewhere to go. If a pipe has already burst, shut off the main water before doing anything else.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="never-indoors" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. What Should Never Be Used to Heat a House Indoors?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Generators, grills, camp stoves, ovens, outdoor patio heaters and any heater not labeled for indoor residential use. Every winter storm produces the same headlines from the same short list of causes.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Generators</h3>

          <p>
            Never run a portable generator inside a home, garage, basement, crawl space or shed, even with doors and windows open. Run it outdoors, at least 20 feet from the house, with the exhaust aimed away from doors, windows and vents. A garage with the door up still counts as inside. Generators make carbon monoxide fast enough to kill within minutes in an enclosed space.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Grills, camp stoves, patio heaters and ovens</h3>

          <p>
            Never heat indoors with a charcoal or gas grill, a camp stove, a turkey fryer or an outdoor patio heater. None of them has an oxygen depletion sensor, and a lit charcoal grill in particular produces large amounts of CO with no visible smoke. Never warm a room with a gas oven or range either; they are not built for it, and an oven left open for hours can produce dangerous CO levels. Candles carry a fire risk out of all proportion to the heat they give.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Garage and job-site heaters</h3>

          <p>
            Heaters sold for garages, workshops and construction sites, including forced-air propane torpedo heaters and many tank-top heaters, are designed and labeled for well-ventilated spaces. The label is what decides. If a heater does not state that it is approved for indoor residential use, treat it as an outdoor heater, however a listing or a forum describes it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What about a fireplace or wood stove?</h3>

          <p>
            One you already use is a different case. With an inspected chimney and a stove or fireplace in good condition, it is an excellent outage heat source, but a storm is no time to experiment with one you have not lit in years. A cracked flue or a chimney fire mid-storm, with emergency services stretched thin, stacks one disaster on top of another.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="choosing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Which Indoor Heater Should You Buy First?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy the alarm, then the sleeping bags, then a Portable Buddy, in that order. The sequence matters because the first two purchases protect everyone whether or not a heater ever gets lit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Start with the alarm and the sleeping bags</h3>

          <p>
            Get a battery-powered CO alarm such as the First Alert CO400 for every level of the house and every sleeping area, plus one more for the room where the heater will run. If you do nothing else on this list, do this. Next, buy one cold-rated sleeping bag, such as the Coleman North Rim, for each person in the household. Those two purchases handle the most dangerous and the most common outage problems with no fuel or power at all.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which Buddy heater fits your room?</h3>

          <p>
            For real heat, the Mr. Heater Portable Buddy is the right first heater for most homes. It warms a closed bedroom, runs on cylinders that store for years, and carries the safety devices that make indoor use reasonable. If the warm room is a living room or the household is large, pick the Big Buddy and plan to run it on low or medium after the first warm-up. If you only need to take the chill off a bathroom or small bedroom, or want a second unit, the Little Buddy fits. Stock enough 1-lb cylinders for several days at a few hours of use a day.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Already own a power station or generator?</h3>

          <p>
            With a power station, add a heated throw like Sunbeam's to the kit and plan to spend stored power on people, not rooms. With a generator or a large home battery, a small ceramic heater like the Lasko 754200 makes sense as clean electric heat for one room. On a typical portable station, skip it. Still deciding how to back up the house? Our guides to the <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">best home generators</a> and the <a href="/best-portable-chargers-for-power-outages/">best portable chargers for power outages</a> cover the rest of the kit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Write the plan down</h3>

          <p>
            Last, put the plan on paper and walk through it once before the cold arrives. Know which room you will use, where the cylinders and alarms live, how to shut off the water, and which temperature or forecast sends you to a warming center or a relative's house instead. The heater is the least important part of that plan. Knowing when to use it, and when to leave, matters more.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes With Emergency Heat
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running Any Heater Without a Battery CO Alarm</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Hardwired CO alarms without battery backup go quiet during an outage, exactly when fuel-burning heaters move indoors. An indoor-rated heater is only one part of the safety system. Place a battery-powered alarm in the same room and near every sleeping area, and test it, before you light anything at all.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using an Outdoor Heater, Grill or Generator Inside</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Patio heaters, tank-top heaters, torpedo heaters, grills, camp stoves and generators are not indoor appliances, whether a window is open or the garage door is up. They have no oxygen depletion sensor and can produce lethal carbon monoxide within minutes. Unless the label says indoor residential use, it stays outside.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Fresh-Air Opening</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Indoor-rated propane heaters still consume oxygen, and their manuals call for a fresh-air opening of a set size. Sealing the room tight to trap heat defeats the design and can trip the oxygen sensor, or worse. Open the window or door by the amount the manual specifies every single time the heater runs.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trying to Heat a Room From a Power Station</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 1,500-watt heater drains a 1kWh power station in roughly 35 to 40 minutes, and an hour later the room is cold again. Put stored energy into heated blankets, which draw a small fraction as much, and into the fridge, lights and medical devices that cannot run on anything else.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Heating the Whole House Instead of One Room</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A small propane heater cannot keep pace with an open-plan floor, so it chews through cylinders while the house stays cold. Pick one small interior room, close it off, block the drafts and hang blankets over the windows. A Portable Buddy can make that room comfortable on a fraction of the fuel.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the safest indoor heater for a power outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The safest choices burn nothing: cold-rated sleeping bags, layered clothing and a heated blanket running from a power station. For real room heat without electricity, pick a propane heater its maker rates for indoor use, such as the Mr. Heater Buddy series with its oxygen depletion sensor and tip-over shutoff. Always run it with a battery-powered CO alarm and the ventilation the manual requires.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Mr. Heater Buddy really safe to use indoors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mr. Heater markets the Buddy series as indoor-safe, and each heater has an oxygen depletion sensor and a tip-over shutoff. Indoor use is reasonable if you follow the manual: open the specified fresh-air gap, keep the stated clearance from anything that burns, use 1-lb cylinders inside, and keep a working battery CO alarm in the room. Many safety agencies advise switching it off before sleep.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a 1-lb propane cylinder last in a Buddy heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 1-lb cylinder holds a little over 21,000 BTU. Burning 9,000 BTU an hour, that comes to roughly two to two and a half hours; at 4,000 BTU it is about five hours. The 3,800 BTU Little Buddy gets about five to six hours. Cold weather and partly used cylinders cut those times, so keep more cylinders on hand than the math suggests.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run a space heater on a portable power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, though only briefly. A 1,500-watt heater empties a 1kWh station in roughly 35 to 40 minutes, and a low setting stretches that to only about an hour. The same energy goes much further in a heated blanket, which can run for most of a night on that battery. Save space heaters for generators or large whole-home batteries.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I heat my house without electricity?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Focus on one small room rather than the whole house. Close it off, block drafts, cover the windows and bring everyone in. While people are awake, run an indoor-rated propane heater with a CO alarm and the required ventilation, then let cold-rated sleeping bags and layered clothing carry the night. A wood stove or fireplace you already have also works if it is inspected and sound.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use my gas oven or stove to heat the house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Gas ovens and ranges are not built to be space heaters, and running one for hours with the door open can produce dangerous carbon monoxide levels while adding a fire risk. Camp stoves and grills of every type fall in the same category. Heat only with appliances labeled for indoor residential use, and keep a battery CO alarm working whenever anything burns indoors.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a kerosene heater for power outages?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Kerosene heaters are not on this list. Whether one may be used indoors depends on state and local rules, the fuel needs careful storage and outdoor refueling, and the heaters demand generous ventilation. For most homes, an indoor-rated propane heater on 1-lb cylinders is simpler to store and run safely. If you are weighing kerosene anyway, read your local codes and the manual first.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

